# GD-1 (round 19, writer 10): was L5_17_export_keyed_fill; what stays is L §5.12 "A keyed fill's output is F[y].taken[…]" read by a def and asks.
# Its regime gains is unboxed (the items at top level, gains.x written x); the
# figures are unchanged. The comment below is the old test's.
# MC-X-F8. L §2.3 post = atom {"[" expr {"," expr} "]" | "." FIELD} …, so keys may follow a field; L §5.12 "A keyed
# fill's output is F[y].taken[…]"; L §5.17: an item R lists in export "any item may read, in another regime or outside
# every regime"; §2.5 puts a keyed item's keys after the qualified id. The regime gains exports the keyed fill
# matched[p]; outside it, the def took and the ask lines read matched[marek].taken[l, c]. Hand-checked from L
# §5.12: marek's supplies l1 = 3, l2 = 5, demand c1 = 4, arcs ordered by l (index order): l1 gives 3, l2 gives 1, so
# took = 4. snap10 refused the load (LOAD-PARSE … matched[marek].taken is not read with keys).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2024-12-31
  start 2024-01-01
  end 2024-12-31

source s "s"
index ps = [marek]
index ls = [l1, l2]
index cs = [c1]

input sup[p in ps, l in ls]: num
input dem[p in ps, c in cs]: num
fill matched[p in ps]
  supply sup[p, l] for l in ls
  demand dem[p, c] for c in cs
  arc true
  order l

def took: num = matched[marek].taken[l1, c1] + matched[marek].taken[l2, c1]

ask q: took
ask r: matched[marek].taken[l2, c1]
ask t: matched[marek].taken[l, c] for l in ls, c in cs

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2024-01-01
  sup[marek, l1] = 3
  sup[marek, l2] = 5
  dem[marek, c1] = 4
=== expect
q: 4
  cut 2024-12-31
r: 1
  cut 2024-12-31
t (l = l1, c = c1): 3
  cut 2024-12-31
t (l = l2, c = c1): 1
  cut 2024-12-31
